  "yesterday_verification_summary": "Yesterday's Day+0 verification confirmed dry conditions across all models. At Day+3, most models correctly verified rain outcomes except ICON, while at Day+7, GFS correctly caught the dry day while ECMWF and Best Match missed.",
  "narrative_markdown": "## Today's Forecast\n\nScattered thunderstorms and afternoon rain showers are expected today, with peak temperatures reaching 29.8°C / 86°F and overnight lows dropping to 18.8°C / 66°F. Thunder possible from the morning, peaking this evening. Observed conditions through 06:30 recorded no rain, no thunder, a temperature of 24°C / 75°F, sustained winds of 9 km/h (5 kt), and 1/8 cloud cover. Skies remain Overcast early, transitioning to Mostly cloudy through midday and into the evening. Winds blow northerly overnight, turning southwest by midday and west-southwest into the evening, with peak gusts reaching 36.7 km/h (20 kt). Rainfall is forecast at 3.5 mm (0.14 in) with a 65% probability of precipitation. Peak solar UV index reaches 9.2 (very high). Ground air quality sensors report an AQI range of 18 to 34 across active reporting stations, with the highest reading at Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central; model estimates place overall ambient air quality at moderate levels with an AQI of 68.\n\n## Extended Outlook\n\nConditions will be warming through Sunday, with showers possible Friday, dry Saturday, rain possible from Sunday, and thunderstorms likely each day. Over the next three days (Friday through Sunday), skies will be Mostly cloudy on Friday before turning Partly cloudy on Saturday and Sunday. Temperatures peak on Sunday with daytime highs ranging from 31°C / 88°F to 35°C / 95°F across numerical guidance, while overnight lows settle near 19°C / 66°F to 20°C / 68°F. Precipitation probability stands at 55% at Day+3 with light shower potential. Looking ahead to days 4 through 7 (Monday through Thursday), Partly cloudy skies on Monday give way to Mostly cloudy conditions from Tuesday through Thursday. Showers and thunderstorms remain likely through the mid-week period, with rain probability reaching 70% by Day+7 and daily high temperatures moderating back toward 26°C / 79°F to 29°C / 84°F.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\n\nConvective instability builds significantly across the basin today, though models disagree on its intensity. GFS shows minimal CAPE near 190 J/kg, whereas ECMWF, ICON, UKMO, and Best Match indicate strong convective energy ranging from 1560 J/kg up to 2600 J/kg, peaking this evening around 20:00. Thunderstorms are possible from the morning, with the primary threat window opening in the afternoon and evening. Any developing thunderstorm brings the potential for sudden, severe wind gusts well above prevailing baseline winds, regardless of calm background forecasts, alongside localized heavy downpours and frequent lightning.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\n\nWinds over Winam Gulf will run north-northeasterly overnight at 11 km/h (6 kt) gusting 22 km/h (12 kt), then southwesterly by midday at 11 km/h (6 kt) gusting 23 km/h (12 kt), then west-southwesterly into the evening at 19 km/h (10 kt) gusting 29 km/h (16 kt). Peak gusts over open water will reach 32.7 km/h (18 kt). High convective instability over the lake basin raises significant marine hazards from late morning through tonight, with sudden squalls, abrupt wind shifts, localized high waves, and lightning posing hazards to boaters and fishermen.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n\n### Synoptic Overview\n\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the north (1011 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1016 hPa) — a 5 hPa spread, a moderate large-scale gradient. The lower pressure to the north is deepening over the next three days, while pressure falls toward the west and overhead. Regionally across the Nyanza Basin, mean sea-level pressure averages 1013.4 hPa to 1015.7 hPa today, maintaining a moderate local gradient between northern lowland stations and higher terrain to the south. Basin-wide pressure will trend downward by 2 to 3 hPa over the next 72 hours, dropping to 1010–1013 hPa by Sunday. This falling surface pressure and lingering moisture transport off Lake Victoria will enhance convective storm development through the late afternoon and evening hours.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\n\nLong-term model performance across 51 verified days provides a settled picture at Day+0 and Day+7, while Day+3 verification across 16 checks provides enough data for model comparison within a 15-point noise floor. Today, GFS calls for a daytime high of 32.6°C / 91°F, running notably warmer than the multi-model consensus high of 29.8°C / 86°F despite a standing long-term bias of slightly over-forecasting highs; the forecaster call aligns closely with ECMWF (28.9°C) and Best Match (29.4°C). The local met service, Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD), predicts a daytime high of 32.0°C / 90°F, an overnight low of 20.0°C / 68°F, and sunny intervals with no precipitation. This diverges from ECMWF, ICON, and Best Match, which all forecast afternoon and evening rainfall driven by strong convective instability. Ground air quality measurements from reporting stations show Good to Moderate air quality: Kisumu Airport records an AQI of 18 (PM2.5 18.0 µg/m³, PM10 15.0 µg/m³), Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central records an AQI of 34 (PM2.5 34.0 µg/m³, PM10 18.0 µg/m³), and Dunga Beach's most recent reading is stale at 3.5 hours old with an AQI of 58 (PM2.5 58.0 µg/m³, PM10 37.0 µg/m³).",
